<section xml:id="sec-compiling">
  <title>Editing, Compiling, and Running a Java Program</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this section we give a brief overview of how to compile and run a Java program.
      For a more detailed discussion see <xref ref="appendix-jdk">Appendix</xref>.

      We begin with a brief overview of the types of programming environments one might encounter.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <subsection>
    <title>Java Development Environments</title>
    <p>
      Java is owned and maintained by Oracle. It is available free for various platforms, including Linux, Windows, and macOS. 
      Since 1998 Java has gone through many versions. The current version as of this writing is Java 25.  
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything needed to write Java programs <mdash/> except for a text editor <mdash/>  is available for free in the 
      <url href="https://www.oracle.com/java/technologies/downloads/#jdk25-mac" visual="">Java Development Kit (JDK)</url>. 
      The JDK also includes a software package called the Java Runtime Environment (or JRE). This is the software you will
      use to execute Java programs. 
    </p>
    <p>
      There are also several <em>integrated development environments (IDEs)</em> available for developing Java programs.
      IDEs integrate the JDK with a text editor, giving you everything you need in one software package.
      These include the open source <url href="https://www.eclipse.org/ide/" visual="">Eclipse IDE</url> and Oracle's own 
      <url href="https://www.oracle.com/tools/technologies/netbeans-ide.html" visual="">NetBeans IDE</url>. 
    </p>
  </subsection>  
    
    
  <subsection>
    <title>The Command-Line Interface</title>
    <p>
    Many of us are used to working with personal computers through a Graphical User Interface (GUI). This is the typical operating environment you 
    will see from an operating system like Microsoft Windows where you open application programs that present as <em>windows</em>
    and you interact with these applications mainly with a mouse-controlled cursor by clicking on graphical elements of the interface.
    Microsoft windows, as well as other popular operating systems like MacOS and Linux, also offer a Command-Line Interface (CLI) for 
    manipulating the system. This interface is more popular among programmers and other technical folks as it can be easier to use 
    this interface for technical tasks. It's also much easier to use a CLI on a remote computer than a GUI, so being able to use 
    a CLI allows you to easily work with computers you have access to across the internet. 
    </p>
    
    <p>
    You can access the CLI on a Windows 10 or 11 machine by opening one of the Command Prompt, PowerShell, or Windows Terminal applications. 
    For example, press start and type <em>cmd</em>. On a Mac, use spotlight and search for <em>Terminal</em>. On a Linux system it will 
    vary by distribution but will be similar. 
    </p>
    
    <p>Once you have a CLI open and running, you will see a prompt waiting for your command. Prompts will vary by system but often 
    display the path (the current directory of files you're working in) and a $ or > symbol at the end near the cursor. You can type
    commands in and execute them on the CLI. For example, on windows type <em>dir</em> or on MacOS/Linux type <em>ls</em>. After you
    type this command, the system will print to the terminal a list of files in your current (working) directory. Afterward you 
    will be returned to the command-prompt, awaiting another command. In the following sections, you will be introduced to commands
    that can edit, compile, and run Java programs. Below is an example of what issuing a command like this could look like.
    </p>
    <console>
<input>ls</input>
<output>HelloWorld.class  HelloWorld.java</output>
    </console>
  </subsection>
  
  
  <subsection>
    <title>Editing a Program</title>
    <p>
      Any text editor can be used to edit a Java program. 
      Popular CLI editors include <c>nano</c> and <c>vi</c>, which are also available on MacOS and Windows.
      Free GUI MacOS editors include <url href="https://www.barebones.com/products/bbedit/index.html" visual="">BBEdit</url> 
      and <url href="https://support.apple.com/guide/textedit/welcome/mac" visual="">TextEdit</url>, 
      which is also available for <url href="https://textedit.en.lo4d.com/windows" visual="">Windows computers</url>.  
      <url href="https://notepad-plus-plus.org/" visual="">Notepad++</url> is also a popular GUI Windows text editor.
    There are also <term>Integrated Development Environments (IDEs)</term> that include an editor, compiler, and other tools, 
    such as Eclipse, Netbeans, IntelliJ, etc.</p>
    <p> 
      Let's take another look at the HelloWorld program (<xref ref="lstng_helloworld"></xref>).
      After editing the <c>HelloWorld</c> program, it must be stored in a 
      file named <c>HelloWorld.java</c>. 
    </p>
    <principle>
      <title>File Names</title>
      <p>
        A text file that defines a <c>public</c>Java class named <c>ClassName</c> 
        must be saved in a text file named exactly <c>ClassName.java</c>. There can be only one 
        <c>public</c> class per file.
      </p>
    </principle>
    <p>
      The file name must exactly match the public class name. For example, if the file 
      containing the <c>HelloWorld</c> class were named <c>helloworld.java</c> or <c>Helloworld.java</c>,
      that would cause an error. Java would be looking for a file named <c>HelloWorld.java</c>.
    </p>
    <principle>
      <title>Case Sensitivity</title>
      <p>
        Java is case sensitive,
        which means that it treats <c>helloWorld</c> and <c>Helloworld</c> as different names.
      </p>
    </principle>
  </subsection>
  
  
  
  
  
  
  <subsection>
    <title>Compiling a Program</title>
    <p>
      Before you can run a Java source program you have to compile it into Java bytecode -- 
      the intermediate code understood by the Java Virtual Machine (JVM). The compilation step converts
      Java <term>source code</term> into Java <term>bytecode</term>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The Java compiler is named <c>javac</c>.  It is included in the JDK.
      To compile <c>HelloWorld.java</c>, you would simply type the following command on the MacOS or Linux or
      Windows command line, where '$' represents the command prompt:
    </p>
    <console>
<input>javac HelloWorld.java</input>
    </console>
    <p>
      Assuming there are no  errors, <c>javac</c> will create a Java bytecode file 
      named <c>HelloWorld.class</c><mdash/>a file that has the same name as the source file 
      but with the extension <c>class</c> instead of <c>java</c>. By default,
      the bytecode file will be placed in the same directory as the source file.
      After compiling, the directory would look something like this:
    </p>
    <console>
<input>ls</input>
<output>HelloWorld.class  HelloWorld.java</output>
    </console>
    <p>
      If <c>javac</c> detects errors in the Java code, 
      a list of error messages will be printed on the console <mdash/>e.g.:
    </p> 
    <console>
<output>HelloWorld.java:8: error: ';' expected</output>
<output>System.out.println("Hello World!")           //  Output statement</output>
<output>       ^</output>
<output>1 error</output>
    </console>
    <p>
    In this case the error is a missing semicolon on line 8.
    </p>


  </subsection>
  
  
  
  
  <subsection>
    <title>Running a Java Program</title>
    <p>
      Once the program successfully compiles and generates the <c>HelloWorld.class</c>
      bytecode file, it can be executed using the <c>java</c> command, also included in the JDK:
    </p>
    <console>
<input>java HelloWorld</input>
<output>Hello World!</output>
    </console>
    <p>
      As you see (<xref ref="fig_stdout">Figure</xref>), the program prints <q>Hello World!</q> on the console. 
      Note that we do not use the <c>.class</c> extension with the <c>java</c> command,
      just the file's name.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig_stdout">
      <caption>Compiling and running the HelloWorld program.</caption>
      <image width="50%" source="chptr01/stdout.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p> 
      The <c>java</c> command works as follows.  It first loads the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) into the computer's main
      memory.  The JVM then loads the Java bytecode into memory and runs it. 
   </p>
   <p>
      Once a Java program is compiled it can be run on Windows, MacOS, Linux or 
      any other computer system that supports the JVM. This <term>platform 
      independence</term> is one of Java's main strengths. Once compiled, Java bytecode is
      portable. This is not the case for other compiled languages, such as C and C++, which 
      must be compiled separately for each different type of computer.
   </p>

<listing xml:id="hellocanvas">
<caption>The HelloWorldCanvas program.</caption>
 <program language="java"><input>
/** File: HelloWorldCanvas program */
import javax.swing.JFrame; // Import class names
import java.awt.Graphics;
import java.awt.Canvas;
public class HelloWorldCanvas extends Canvas // Class header
{
    // Start of body
    public void paint(Graphics g)  // The paint method
    {
        g.drawString("Hello, World!", 10, 10);
    }  // End of paint

    public static void main(String[] args)   // The main() method
    {   HelloWorldCanvas c = new HelloWorldCanvas();
        JFrame f = new JFrame();
        f.add(c);
        f.setSize(150,150);
        f.setVisible(true);
    }  // End of main
}  // End of HelloWorldCanvas
 </input></program>
</listing>

   <p>
    The steps involved in running the <c>HelloWorldCanvas</c> program (<xref ref="hellocanvas">Figure</xref>) are identical:
 <console><input>javac HelloWorldCanvas.java</input><input>java HelloWorldCanvas</input></console>
</p>

<p>
In this case however, the program's output would not appear on the command line. Instead,
as shown in <xref ref="fig_popup">Figure</xref>, it would
be displayed in a pop-up window, a Java <c>JFrame</c>.
</p>

<p>    
    <figure xml:id="fig_popup">
      <caption>Output from the HelloWorldCanvas program.</caption>
      <image width="50%" source="chptr01/helloCanvasOutput.png"/>
    </figure>
</p>
<activity label="compiling_hello_world_canvas">
  <statement><p>Try the HelloWorldCanvas program below.</p>
      </statement>
      <program label="compiling_hello_world_canvas_practice_prg" interactive="activecode" language="java"  datafile="HtmlImageUtil.java">
    <input>
import java.awt.*;

public class HelloWorldCanvas extends Canvas
{
    public void paint(Graphics g) {
        g.drawString("Hello, World!", 10, 10);
    }//paint()

    public static void main(String[] args){
        HelloWorldCanvas c = new HelloWorldCanvas();
	// instead of using Swing, show to the web page:
        HtmlImageUtil.show(c,150, 50);
    } //main
} //HelloWorldCanvas
    </input>
    </program>

  </activity>
  </subsection>



<subsection xml:id="subsec-repls">
	<title>Working With Java's JShell REPL</title>

    
    <p>
        There are many ways to categorize programming languages. For instance, some programming languages are called <em>object-oriented</em>, <em>functional</em>, or <em>statically typed</em>, to name a few, and many of these categories overlap. An important category, as it relates to teaching Java to a beginning programmer, is that of <term>scripting languages</term>. This is important because Java has never in the past been considered one <footnote><p>Do not confuse Java with Javascript, which is an entirely different language and is indeed a scripting language.</p></footnote>.
    </p>
    <p> A scripting language is one that is intended for quickly writing short, ad-hoc programs that perform a simple task, possibly only once. An important tool for working with a scripting language is a <acronym>REPL</acronym> (Read Evaluate Print Loop). A REPL is much like a CLI as it was described (and indeed runs within a CLI). It allows you to run small snippets of a program, one at a time, and observe what the computer does in real time. This way the programmer can quickly react, correct errors, and experiment. This functionality is of obvious importance to a new programmer learning how to use the language. </p>
    <p> Java has long been infamous for its <em>wordiness</em>. That is, it took a lot of typing to say anything meaningful. In JShell, <em>Hello World!</em> is just one line:
    <figure>
        <title>jshell</title>
        <caption>Using the JShell REPL</caption>
      <image source="chptr01/jshell.png"/>
    </figure>
         You can see in the figure that the first thing JShell did after I started it up in a terminal was to welcome me and indicate what version is running. After that you see the command prompt. <console><input>jshell</input></console>  At that prompt I typed <console><input prompt="jshell> ">System.out.println("Hello World!");</input></console> which is a snippet of Java that instructs the computer to print the same message back to me. JShell read this snippet in from me (the R for read in REPL), then did as it was instructed (the E for evaluate and P for Print steps). You can see the JShell REPL complying with that command below and then returning me to the command prompt for another command (L for loop -- after each command, it waits for another, looping indefinitely).
    </p>
    
    <p>
        There are many commands you can enter at the command prompt that are not Java code snippets but are unique to using JShell. A very important one is the <c>/help</c> command. Typing in this command causes JShell to report on all the other special commands available to you. Another important command is <c>/exit</c>, which terminates JShell. We will see other commands as they become relevant in later sections. 
    </p>
</subsection>
</section>
